WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.580
We have an interesting question on our Quiz,
where the majority was wrong.

00:00:03.580 --> 00:00:07.740
It’s about convolutions and the difference
between invariance and equivariance.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.971
Translational or shift invariance means that
irrespectively of the position of a cat, the

00:00:11.971 --> 00:00:14.860
output of the convolution operation is the
same.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:18.640
Which it isn’t since the feature map still
reflects the original position.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:23.119
So this is actually equivariance because when
the position of the cat changes, the output

00:00:23.119 --> 00:00:25.660
changes in the same way, reflecting shift.

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.689
And with the sliding kernel, this is exactly
what happens.

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:32.000
So convolutions are not shift invariant, but
equivariant.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.380
If you got this wrong, it’s okay because
these words are used interchangeably in language

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:38.740
(except of highly mathematical contexts).

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:42.260
Please mind that the question asks about the
convolution only.

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:46.730
It was the next day’s trick question inquiring
about the CNN as a whole.

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:51.480
While convolutional layers do not make the
CNN shift invariant but only equivariant,

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:55.390
it takes spatial pooling layers to make it
approximately shift invariant

00:00:55.390 --> 00:00:59.780
by pushing information that was far away,
into approximately the same spot.

